SEC. 70401: ENHANCEMENT OF EMPLOYER-PROVIDED CHILD CARE CREDIT.

This section increases the employer-provided child care credit from 25% to 40% of qualified child care expenditures, and to 50% for eligible small businesses (those meeting a 5-year gross receipts test). The maximum annual credit is raised from $150,000 to $500,000, or $600,000 for eligible small businesses, with both amounts subject to inflation adjustment starting in 2027. The credit is expanded to cover child care services provided through intermediate entities that contract with qualified facilities, and facilities jointly owned or operated by the taxpayer and other persons are now eligible. The changes apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70402: ENHANCEMENT OF ADOPTION CREDIT.

This section makes up to $5,000 of the adoption credit refundable, meaning it can be received as a refund even if it exceeds tax liability. The inflation adjustment rules for the credit are updated, with the refundable portion's adjustment based on a 2025 start year and 2024 base year. The carryforward rules are modified to apply only to the non-refundable portion of the credit. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

SEC. 70403: RECOGNIZING INDIAN TRIBAL GOVERNMENTS FOR PURPOSES OF DETERMINING WHETHER A CHILD HAS SPECIAL NEEDS FOR PURPOSES OF THE ADOPTION CREDIT.

This section amends the definition of a child with special needs for the adoption credit to include determinations made by an Indian tribal government, in addition to a State. This allows adoptive parents of children determined to have special needs by a tribal government to claim the credit. The change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

SEC. 70404: ENHANCEMENT OF THE DEPENDENT CARE ASSISTANCE PROGRAM.

This section increases the exclusion from gross income for employer-provided dependent care assistance from $5,000 to $7,500 per year (and from $2,500 to $3,750 for married individuals filing separately). This change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70405: ENHANCEMENT OF CHILD AND DEPENDENT CARE TAX CREDIT.

This section increases the maximum child and dependent care tax credit percentage from 35% to 50% of qualifying expenses. The credit percentage is reduced by 1 percentage point for each $2,000 of adjusted gross income (AGI) over $15,000, down to a floor of 35%. It is further reduced by 1 percentage point for each $2,000 of AGI over $75,000 (or $4,000 over $150,000 for joint filers), down to a floor of 20%. This change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70411: TAX CREDIT FOR CONTRIBUTIONS OF INDIVIDUALS TO SCHOLARSHIP GRANTING ORGANIZATIONS.

This section creates a new nonrefundable tax credit of up to $1,700 per year for individual cash contributions to state-listed "scholarship granting organizations" that fund K-12 scholarships for eligible students (household income up to 300% of area median gross income). The credit is reduced by any state tax credit claimed for the same contribution, and the contribution cannot also be claimed as a charitable deduction. Unused credits can be carried forward for up to 5 years. The credit applies to taxable years ending after December 31, 2026, and the related exclusion for scholarship amounts from gross income applies to amounts received after that date.

SEC. 70412: EXCLUSION FOR EMPLOYER PAYMENTS OF STUDENT LOANS.

This section makes permanent the exclusion from an employee's gross income for employer payments of up to $5,250 per year toward the employee's student loans, removing the previous expiration date of January 1, 2026. The $5,250 limit is now subject to an inflation adjustment, rounded to the nearest $50, starting in 2027. This change applies to payments made after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70413: ADDITIONAL EXPENSES TREATED AS QUALIFIED HIGHER EDUCATION EXPENSES FOR PURPOSES OF 529 ACCOUNTS.

This section expands the definition of qualified higher education expenses for 529 plans to include K-12 expenses such as tuition, curriculum materials, books, online materials, tutoring (with specific instructor qualifications), standardized test fees, dual enrollment fees, and educational therapies for students with disabilities. The annual limit on distributions for K-12 tuition is increased from $10,000 to $20,000 per beneficiary. The expansion of expense types applies to distributions made after enactment, while the increased limit applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70414: CERTAIN POSTSECONDARY CREDENTIALING EXPENSES TREATED AS QUALIFIED HIGHER EDUCATION EXPENSES FOR PURPOSES OF 529 ACCOUNTS.

This section expands the definition of qualified higher education expenses for 529 plans to include expenses for "recognized postsecondary credential programs," such as tuition, fees, books, supplies, and required testing for industry-recognized credentials, apprenticeships, and occupational licenses. The program must be on a state list, in a Department of Veterans Affairs directory, require a recognized exam, or be identified by the Treasury Secretary. This change applies to distributions made after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70415: MODIFICATION OF EXCISE TAX ON INVESTMENT INCOME OF CERTAIN PRIVATE COLLEGES AND UNIVERSITIES.

This section replaces the flat 1.4% excise tax on net investment income of private colleges with a tiered rate structure based on "student adjusted endowment" (assets per student). The new rates are 1.4% for endowments of $500,000 to $750,000 per student, 4% for $750,000 to $2,000,000, and 8% for over $2,000,000. The definition of net investment income is expanded to include student loan interest and certain federally-subsidized royalty income. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70416: EXPANDING APPLICATION OF TAX ON EXCESS COMPENSATION WITHIN TAX-EXEMPT ORGANIZATIONS.

This section expands the definition of "covered employee" for the 21% excise tax on excess compensation to include any employee of an applicable tax-exempt organization, not just the top five highest-compensated employees. The change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70421: PERMANENT RENEWAL AND ENHANCEMENT OF OPPORTUNITY ZONES.

This section makes the Opportunity Zone program permanent, with new designations every 10 years starting July 1, 2026, and removes the special rule for Puerto Rico. The definition of a low-income community is revised to include tracts with a poverty rate of at least 20% and a median family income up to 125% of the area median. The capital gains deferral period is shortened to 5 years, and the basis step-up for investments held 5 years is increased to 10% (30% for investments in "qualified rural opportunity funds"). New information reporting requirements and penalties are established for funds and businesses. The changes apply to investments made after December 31, 2026, with some exceptions.

SEC. 70422: PERMANENT ENHANCEMENT OF LOW-INCOME HOUSING TAX CREDIT.

This section makes permanent the increased state housing credit ceiling for the Low-Income Housing Tax Credit, setting the per-capita amount at $1.12 (previously a temporary higher amount). It also modifies the tax-exempt bond financing requirement, allowing a project to qualify for the credit if at least 25% of its basis is financed with tax-exempt bonds issued after December 31, 2025, provided those bonds finance at least 5% of the basis. The changes apply to calendar years and buildings placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70423: PERMANENT EXTENSION OF NEW MARKETS TAX CREDIT.

This section makes the New Markets Tax Credit permanent by removing the sunset date of 2025, allowing the $5 billion annual allocation to continue for each calendar year after 2019. It also modifies the carryover rules for unused allocation authority, limiting the carryover to 5 years and treating any pre-2026 excess as occurring in 2025. The changes apply to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70424: PERMANENT AND EXPANDED REINSTATEMENT OF PARTIAL DEDUCTION FOR CHARITABLE CONTRIBUTIONS OF INDIVIDUALS WHO DO NOT ELECT TO ITEMIZE.

This section makes permanent the above-the-line charitable deduction for non-itemizers and increases the maximum deduction from $300 to $1,000 for individuals and from $600 to $2,000 for married couples filing jointly. The change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70425: 0.5 PERCENT FLOOR ON DEDUCTION OF CONTRIBUTIONS MADE BY INDIVIDUALS.

This section introduces a new floor for individual charitable deductions, disallowing the deduction for the first 0.5% of a taxpayer's contribution base. The floor is applied to contributions in a specific order (e.g., 50% limit organizations first, then 30% limit organizations, etc.). Disallowed amounts due to the floor can be carried forward to future years. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70426: 1-PERCENT FLOOR ON DEDUCTION OF CHARITABLE CONTRIBUTIONS MADE BY CORPORATIONS.

This section introduces a new floor for corporate charitable deductions, disallowing the deduction for the first 1% of a corporation's taxable income. The deduction is still limited to 10% of taxable income, and the carryforward rules are modified to allow a 5-year carryforward for amounts disallowed by the 10% limit, including amounts disallowed by the new 1% floor. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70427: PERMANENT INCREASE IN LIMITATION ON COVER OVER OF TAX ON DISTILLED SPIRITS.

This section permanently increases the limitation on the "cover over" of excise taxes on distilled spirits to Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands from $13.25 to $13.50 per proof gallon. The change applies to distilled spirits brought into the United States after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70428: NONPROFIT COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT ACTIVITIES IN REMOTE NATIVE VILLAGES.

This section clarifies that for tax-exempt entities identified in the Magnuson-Stevens Act, activities related to participation or investment in Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands fisheries are considered substantially related to their exempt purpose. It also provides for tax-free treatment of certain asset transfers from wholly-owned subsidiaries to these entities, and exempts income from the transferred business. The provision is effective upon enactment and remains in effect during the existence of the western Alaska community development quota program.

SEC. 70429: ADJUSTMENT OF CHARITABLE DEDUCTION FOR CERTAIN EXPENSES INCURRED IN SUPPORT OF NATIVE ALASKAN SUBSISTENCE WHALING.

This section increases the maximum charitable deduction for expenses incurred in support of Native Alaskan subsistence whaling from $10,000 to $50,000. The change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70430: EXCEPTION TO PERCENTAGE OF COMPLETION METHOD OF ACCOUNTING FOR CERTAIN RESIDENTIAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACTS.

This section expands the exception to the percentage-of-completion method of accounting to include all "residential construction contracts," not just "home construction contracts." For residential contracts that are not home construction contracts, the exception applies if the contract is completed within 3 years (instead of 2 years). The change applies to contracts entered into in taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70431: EXPANSION OF QUALIFIED SMALL BUSINESS STOCK GAIN EXCLUSION.

This section increases the exclusion for gain from qualified small business stock (QSBS) held for at least 3 years, with the exclusion percentage rising from 50% to 75% for stock held 4 years and 100% for stock held 5 years or more. The per-issuer limit on eligible gain is increased from $10 million to $15 million for stock acquired after the enactment date, with inflation adjustment. The aggregate gross asset limit for a corporation to be a qualified small business is increased from $50 million to $75 million. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after enactment, with the asset limit applying to stock issued after enactment.

SEC. 70432: REPEAL OF REVISION TO DE MINIMIS RULES FOR THIRD PARTY NETWORK TRANSACTIONS.

This section reinstates the pre-2021 de minimis threshold for third-party settlement organizations (like PayPal or Venmo) to report transactions to the IRS, raising the reporting threshold back to over $20,000 in aggregate payments and over 200 transactions per year. It also aligns backup withholding rules with this threshold. The change is retroactive to the American Rescue Plan Act and applies to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2024.

SEC. 70433: INCREASE IN THRESHOLD FOR REQUIRING INFORMATION REPORTING WITH RESPECT TO CERTAIN PAYEES.

This section increases the threshold for businesses to file Form 1099-NEC for payments to independent contractors from $600 to $2,000 per calendar year. The threshold is subject to inflation adjustment starting in 2027. The change applies to payments made after December 31, 2025.

SEC. 70434: TREATMENT OF CERTAIN QUALIFIED SOUND RECORDING PRODUCTIONS.

This section extends the tax treatment for qualified film and television productions to "qualified sound recording productions" (sound recordings produced and recorded in the United States). This allows for the immediate expensing of up to $150,000 of production costs and makes them eligible for bonus depreciation. The change applies to productions commencing in taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70435: EXCLUSION OF INTEREST ON LOANS SECURED BY RURAL OR AGRICULTURAL REAL PROPERTY.

This section creates a new exclusion from gross income for 25% of the interest received by qualified lenders (banks, insurance companies, and certain holding company subsidiaries) on loans secured by rural or agricultural real estate. The loan must be made after the date of enactment and to a non-foreign entity. The change applies to taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70436: REDUCTION OF TRANSFER AND MANUFACTURING TAXES FOR CERTAIN DEVICES.

This section reduces the transfer and making taxes on firearms from $200 to $0 for all firearms except machineguns and destructive devices, which remain at $200. The change applies to calendar quarters beginning more than 90 days after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70437: TREATMENT OF CAPITAL GAINS FROM THE SALE OF CERTAIN FARMLAND PROPERTY.

This section allows taxpayers to elect to pay the net income tax attributable to the sale of qualified farmland property to a qualified farmer in 4 equal annual installments. The farmland must have been used for farming for 10 years and be subject to a covenant restricting its use to farming for 10 years after the sale. The change applies to sales or exchanges in taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

SEC. 70438: EXTENSION OF RULES FOR TREATMENT OF CERTAIN DISASTER-RELATED PERSONAL CASUALTY LOSSES.

This section extends the special rules for the treatment of disaster-related personal casualty losses by substituting the date of enactment of this Act for the original date in the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2020. This effectively extends the favorable treatment for such losses to disasters occurring after the original date.

SEC. 70439: RESTORATION OF TAXABLE REIT SUBSIDIARY ASSET TEST.

This section increases the allowable percentage of assets that a Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) can hold in taxable REIT subsidiaries from 20% to 25% of the REIT's total assets. The change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


